vering by \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n3. **Obtaining all smaller values of \\(k\\)**  \n\n   Starting from the maximal construction, replace any sunny line by a non\u2011sunny line (vertical, horizontal or diagonal) that passes through all points of the replaced line.  \n   Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.  \n   Repeating this operation yields configurations for every integer \\(k\\) between the maximal admissible value and \\(0\\).\n\n   Since the case \\(k=0\\) is trivially realised by the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\), all intermediate values are attainable.\n\nCombining the upper bound with the explicit constructions gives the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line then \\(b=pa+q\\).  \nFor another integer point \\((a',b')\\) on the same line we have  \n\n\\[\nb'-b=p(a'-a).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\neq-1\\), the quantity \\(a'+b'-(a+b)= (1-p)(a'-a)\\) has the same sign as \\(a'-a\\).  \nConsequently, moving from one lattice point of the line to the next changes the sum \\(a+b\\) by at least \\(|1-p|\\ge 2\\) (the equality \\(|1-p|=1\\) would give \\(p=0\\) or \\(p=2\\), both forbidden; the case \\(|1-p|=0\\) would give \\(p=1\\), which is allowed, but then the line is parallel to the diagonal \\(x-y=0\\) and still satisfies the hypothesis \u2013 we treat it separately below).  \n\nIf \\(p=1\\) the line has equation \\(y=x+q\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the condition \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) forces  \n\n\\[\n2a+q\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1-q}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integer points can lie on such a line.  \n\nIf \\(p\\neq1\\) then each step in the integer lattice changes \\(a+b\\) by at least \\(2\\); the smallest possible value of \\(a+b\\) for a point of the line is at least \\(2\\), the largest at most \\(n+1\\). Hence the number of admissible points is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1)-2}{2}+1=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 2  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny then  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nEquality holds iff \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n*Proof.*  \nInsert the bound of Lemma\u202f1 and compute  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nn\\frac{n+1}{2}=|T_n|, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\frac{n}{2}=|T_n|-\\frac n2<|T_n|, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\nConsequently, for even \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (maximal constructions)\n\n*Even \\(n=2t\\).*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_0 : x=1 \\qquad\\text{(non\u2011sunny)},\\\\\n&\\ell_j : y=-2x+(2t+2-j)\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,2t-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_j\\) has slope \\(-2\\) and therefore is sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the integer solutions of \\(\\ell_j\\) inside the triangle satisfy  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le t,\\qquad b=-2a+(2t+2-j)\\ge1,\n\\]\n\nhence exactly the \\(t\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(a,\\;2t+2-j-2a),\\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,t .\n\\]\n\nThese points are distinct for different values of \\(j\\); together with the \\(2t\\) points of \\(\\ell_0\\) they form the whole set \\(T_{2t}\\).  \nThus we have a covering with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2t+1\\).*  \nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,2t+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_j : y=-2x+(2t+3-j).\n\\]\n\nExactly as above, each \\(L_j\\) contains the \\(t+1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(a,\\;2t+3-j-2a),\\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1,\n\\]\n\nand the families are pairwise disjoint.  \nSince \\((t+1)(2t+1)=|T_{2t+1}|\\), the \\(2t+1\\) lines \\(L_j\\) cover all points; all are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (downgrading sunny lines)\n\nLet a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines be given.  \nIf we replace a sunny line by any non\u2011sunny line that passes through **all** points of the replaced line (possible because a non\u2011sunny line can be chosen with the same two points, e.g. a vertical line through the leftmost point), the covering property is preserved and the number of sunny lines decreases by one.\n\nConsequently, from a configuration with the maximal admissible number of sunny lines we obtain configurations for every smaller non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n*Existence.*  \nFor odd \\(n\\) Lemma\u202f3 gives a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines; Lemma\u202f4 yields configurations for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor even \\(n\\) Lemma\u202f3 gives a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines; Lemma\u202f4 again yields configurations for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n*Non\u2011existence.*  \nCorollary\u202f2 shows that for even \\(n\\) a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines would require more points than a sunny line can provide, which is impossible. Hence \\(k=n\\) is excluded when \\(n\\) is even. No other restriction appears, because for every admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited a construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
