            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (points on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and \\(q\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) then \\(b=pa+q\\).  \nFor another lattice point \\((a',b')\\in\\ell\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb'-b=p(a'-a).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n(a'+b')-(a+b)= (1-p)(a'-a). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq-1\\), the factor \\(|1-p|\\) is a positive integer at least \\(2\\) (the only possibilities for \\(|1-p|=1\\) would be \\(p=0\\) or \\(p=2\\); \\(p=0\\) is forbidden, and \\(p=2\\) would give a line parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\) after a rotation of the axes, which is still sunny – we treat it separately below).  \n\nIf \\(p=2\\) then (1) gives \\((a'+b')-(a+b)= -a'+a\\); moving from one lattice point to the next changes the sum \\(a+b\\) by exactly \\(\\pm1\\). In this special case the line has equation \\(y=2x+q\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(a\\ge1\\) forces  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-q}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points occur.\n\nOtherwise \\(|1-p|\\ge2\\). From (1) the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by at least \\(2\\) when we move from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to another. Since the smallest possible value of \\(a+b\\) for a point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) is \\(2\\) and the largest is \\(n+1\\), the number of admissible points is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1)-2}{2}+1=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 2 (global bound)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny then  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nEquality holds exactly when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n*Proof.*  \nInsert the bound of Lemma 1 and compute  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nn\\frac{n+1}{2}=|T_n|, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\frac{n}{2}=|T_n|-\\frac n2<|T_n|, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\nHence for even \\(n\\) a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible; at most \\(n-1\\) of the lines can be sunny.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (maximal constructions)\n\n*Even \\(n=2t\\).*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_0:\\;x=1\\qquad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;y=-2x+(2t+2-j)\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,2t-1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_j\\) has slope \\(-2\\) and therefore is sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the integer points of \\(\\ell_j\\) satisfying \\(a+b\\le2t+1\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,\\;2t+2-j-2a\\bigr),\\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,t .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_j\\) contains precisely \\(t\\) points of \\(T_{2t}\\).  \nThe families \\(\\{\\ell_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together with the \\(2t\\) points of \\(\\ell_0\\) they give  \n\n\\[\n(2t-1)\\cdot t+2t=2t^2-t=|T_{2t}|.\n\\]\n\nHence we obtain a covering with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2t+1\\).*  \nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,2t+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;y=-2x+(2t+3-j).\n\\]\n\nAgain each line is sunny.  \nFor a given \\(j\\) the admissible integer points are  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,\\;2t+3-j-2a\\bigr),\\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently each \\(L_j\\) contains \\(t+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_{2t+1}\\).  \nThe \\(2t+1\\) families are disjoint and  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1)=|T_{2t+1}|,\n\\]\n\nso all points are covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (downgrading sunny lines)\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) lines satisfy the covering condition and contain \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nIf we replace a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) by a non‑sunny line that passes through **all** points of \\(\\ell\\) (such a line exists because any two distinct points determine a line, and we may choose the vertical, horizontal or diagonal line among the three possibilities that contains those two points), the covering property is preserved and the number of sunny lines decreases by one.\n\nRepeating this operation we can obtain configurations with any smaller non‑negative number of sunny lines.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n*Existence.*  \n\n- If \\(n\\) is odd, Lemma 3 provides a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  \n  By Lemma 4 we obtain configurations for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n- If \\(n\\) is even, Lemma 3 gives a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n  Lemma 4 then yields configurations for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n*Non‑existence.*  \n\nCorollary 2 shows that for even \\(n\\) a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines would require more points than a sunny line can provide; therefore \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is even.  \nNo other restriction appears, because for every admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited a construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
