s_1\\) pick a point \\(P=(a,b)\\); from \\(x+y=s_2\\) pick a point \\(Q=(c,d)\\) with \\(a\\neq c\\) and \\(b\\neq d\\) (possible because each diagonal contains at least two points with distinct \\(x\\)\u2013coordinates).  \n     The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is **sunny**: its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{d-b}{c-a}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) lie on different anti\u2011diagonals.  \n\n     Replace the two anti\u2011diagonal lines \\(x+y=s_1\\) and \\(x+y=s_2\\) by the single sunny line \\(\\ell\\) **and** keep all the remaining \\(n-2\\) anti\u2011diagonal lines.  \n     The total number of lines is now \\((n-2)+(1)+(1)=n\\) (the extra \u201c\\(1\\)\u201d is the original sunny line count \\(k\\)).  \n     The set of points covered is unchanged: every point of the two removed diagonals lies on \\(\\ell\\) (by construction) and all other points are still on the unchanged anti\u2011diagonals.  \n     Consequently we have produced a configuration for the same \\(n\\) with **\\(k+1\\)** sunny lines.\n\n   Applying (A) repeatedly yields configurations for \\(n+1\\) with the same \\(k\\); applying (B) once after (A) yields configurations for \\(n+1\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  By induction we obtain, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions for the base case together with the inductive operations prove that **all** integers \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) are attainable, and no other values are possible because we have only \\(n\\) lines. Hence the answer is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or anti\u2011diagonal \\((x+y=c)\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n------------