which is non\u2011sunny.  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered by the original \\(n\\) lines, and the new line covers precisely the points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{L_{new}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a valid configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one (operation B)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) be a configuration for \\(n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\), among the anti\u2011diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) there exist at least two distinct sums, say \\(s_1\\) and \\(s_2\\), whose diagonals each contain at least two points.  \nChoose  \n\n\\[\nP=(a,b)\\in\\{x+y=s_1\\},\\qquad Q=(c,d)\\in\\{x+y=s_2\\}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(a\\neq c\\) and \\(b\\neq d\\) (possible because each diagonal contains points with different \\(x\\)\u2013coordinates).  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=\\text{the line through }P\\text{ and }Q .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) lie on different anti\u2011diagonals, the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{d-b}{c-a}\\neq0,\\ \\infty,\\ -1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(\\ell\\) is **sunny**.\n\nNow form a new family for the same \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'_n(k+1)=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\setminus\\{x+y=s_1,\\;x+y=s_2\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell\\}.\n\\]\n\n*The number of lines.*  \nWe removed two lines and added one, so the total is still \\(n\\).  \nThe number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one, because \\(\\ell\\) is sunny and the two removed lines were non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Coverage of points.*  \nEvery point of the two removed diagonals lies on \\(\\ell\\) (by construction).  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered by the unchanged lines.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L'_n(k+1)\\) satisfies the required covering property.\n\nConsequently, from a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain one with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines, without changing the total number of lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Completing the induction\n\nStarting from the base case \\(n=3\\) (where all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are known to be possible), we apply operation **A** repeatedly to increase \\(n\\) while preserving any chosen \\(k\\).  \nTo obtain configurations with a larger number of sunny lines, we apply operation **B** once (or repeatedly) after each application of **A**.  \n\nFormally, for any \\(n\\ge3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we construct a configuration as follows:\n\n1. Begin with the base configuration for \\(n=3\\) having \\(k_0=\\min\\{k,3\\}\\) sunny lines.  \n2. While the current number of points is less than \\(n\\):  \n   * apply **A** to increase the size of the point set (adds one line, does not change the sunny count);  \n   * if the current sunny count is still smaller than the desired \\(k\\), apply **B** to raise it by one.  \n\nBecause each application of **A** increases the number of points by exactly one diagonal (i.e. by one line) and each application of **B** raises the sunny count by one without changing the total number of lines, after finitely many steps we reach the target size \\(n\\) and the target sunny count \\(k\\).  \n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe have exactly \\(n\\) lines.  The number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\), and it cannot be negative.  Hence no integer outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\