011diagonal \\(D\\) (otherwise we replace it by an anti\u2011diagonal without affecting the number of sunny lines).  \n\n   The anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) contains the even number \\(n-1\\) of points \\(P_i\\) (4). Because a sunny line cannot contain two points of the same anti\u2011diagonal, each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must contain **exactly one** of the points \\(P_i\\). Consequently every sunny line is determined by the choice of its point on \\(x+y=n\\).  \n\n   Consider the remaining points of \\(T_n\\), i.e. those not on \\(D\\) and not on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=n\\). They all lie on anti\u2011diagonals with sums \\(s\\) where \\(2\\le s\\le n-1\\). For a fixed \\(s\\) the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points, all of which have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates. Because the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines already have distinct slopes (they are forced to be distinct, otherwise two of them would meet the same point of \\(x+y=n\\) and would be identical), at most one of those lines can pass through a given point of the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=s\\). Hence the total number of points of the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(s-1\\).  \n\n   Adding over all \\(s=2,\\dots ,n-1\\) we obtain that the sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{n-1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points outside \\(D\\) and \\(x+y=n\\).  \n   However the set \\(T_n\\setminus\\bigl(D\\cup\\{x+y=n\\}\\bigr)\\) contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-|D|-|x+y=n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n-(n-1)\n   =\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points \u2013 the same number. Thus *every* point of those anti\u2011diagonals must be hit by a sunny line. In particular, for each sum \\(s\\) the \\(s-1\\) points of the anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) must be distributed among the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, one point per line.  \n\n   Now fix two consecutive anti\u2011diagonals, say \\(x+y=s\\) and \\(x+y=s+1\\) (with \\(2\\l