            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid k\\neq n-1\\text{ when }n\\text{ is odd}\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words  \n\n* if \\(n\\) is even, **every** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised;  \n* if \\(n\\) is odd, all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) except \\(k=n-1\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n2. **Two elementary constructions.**  \n   *All non‑sunny* – the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals  \n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n   are distinct non‑sunny lines and each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them. Hence \\(k=0\\) is always possible.  \n\n   *All sunny* – for every \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=i\\,x-(i-1) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) is the integer \\(i\\neq0,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   A direct substitution shows that for each \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains the two points  \n   \\[\n   (1,i)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(i,1),\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   both belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \n   Moreover every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in\\{-(n-1),\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); choosing \\(i=\\max\\{a,b\\}\\) we see that \\((a,b)\\) is one of the two points listed in (2) on the line \\(L_i\\). Consequently the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=n\\) is always possible.\n\n3. **The critical anti‑diagonal.**  \n   Points that lie on the same anti‑diagonal have slope \\(-1\\); therefore **no sunny line can contain two distinct points of the same anti‑diagonal**.  \n\n4. **Construction for intermediate values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix a non‑sunny line \\(D\\) to be the largest anti‑diagonal  \n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   It contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The remaining points are those with sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\); the anti‑diagonal with sum \\(n\\) contains \\(n-1\\) points\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,n-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we shall produce \\(k\\) sunny lines that each contain a distinct point of the set \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_{n-1}\\}\\) and, together with the \\(n-k-1\\) remaining anti‑diagonals, cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Choice of the sunny lines.*  \n   Let \\(k\\le n-2\\). For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; \\text{the line through }P_i=(i,n-i)\\text{ and }Q_i=(i+1,1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{1-(n-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n+1=-(n-i-1),\n   \\]\n\n   which is an integer different from \\(0,-1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny and the \\(S_i\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.  \n   The point \\(Q_i\\) belongs to the anti‑diagonal with sum \\(i+2\\) (which is \\(\\le n\\)), therefore each \\(S_i\\) covers **one** point of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=i+2\\) in addition to the point \\(P_i\\).\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) together cover  \n\n   * the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n\\);\n   * the \\(k\\) points \\(Q_1,\\dots ,Q_k\\), each lying on a distinct anti‑diagonal among \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=k+2\\).\n\n   The anti‑diagonals that have not been touched yet are  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=k+3,\\;x+y=k+4,\\dots ,x+y=n .\n   \\]\n\n   We cover each of them by a **non‑sunny** line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); namely we take the remaining anti‑diagonals themselves as lines. Altogether we use  \n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\);\n   * \\(n-k-1\\) non‑sunny anti‑diagonals (all except the one \\(x+y=n+1\\) already fixed as \\(D\\) and the \\(k\\) anti‑diagonals already represented by the points \\(Q_i\\));\n   * the line \\(D\\) itself.\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k-1)+1=n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the chosen lines, as can be checked point‑by‑point from the description above. Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n5. **The case \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\).*  \n   When \\(n\\) is even we can realise \\(k=n-1\\) by the following explicit construction.  \n   Keep the non‑sunny line \\(D\\) of (3). The anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) now contains the even number \\(n-1\\) of points \\(P_i\\) (4). Pair them as \\((P_{2j-1},P_{2j})\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,\\frac{n-1}{2}\\). For each pair define a sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   T_j:\\text{ the line through }P_{2j-1}\\text{ and }(2j,1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(T_j\\) equals \\(-(n-2j)\\), which is an integer different from \\(0,-1\\); thus each \\(T_j\\) is sunny. The \\( \\frac{n-1}{2}\\) lines \\(T_j\\) together contain all points of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) and also \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) distinct points of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=3,5,\\dots ,n-1\\). The remaining anti‑diagonals (those with even sum) are covered by the corresponding non‑sunny anti‑diagonal lines. Counting shows that we have used exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line \\(D\\). Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n   *Odd \\(n\\).*  \n   Suppose that \\(n\\) is odd and that a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists. As before we may assume that the unique non‑sunny line is the largest anti‑diagonal \\(D\\) (otherwise we replace it by an anti‑diagonal without affecting the number of sunny lines).  \n\n   The anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) contains the even number \\(n-1\\) of points \\(P_i\\) (4). Because a sunny line cannot contain two points of the same anti‑diagonal, each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must contain **exactly one** of the points \\(P_i\\). Consequently every sunny line is determined by the choice of its point on \\(x+y=n\\).  \n\n   Consider the remaining points of \\(T_n\\), i.e. those not on \\(D\\) and not on the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n\\). They all lie on anti‑diagonals with sums \\(s\\) where \\(2\\le s\\le n-1\\). For a fixed \\(s\\) the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points, all of which have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates. Because the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines already have distinct slopes (they are forced to be distinct, otherwise two of them would meet the same point of \\(x+y=n\\) and would be identical), at most one of those lines can pass through a given point of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\). Hence the total number of points of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(s-1\\).  \n\n   Adding over all \\(s=2,\\dots ,n-1\\) we obtain that the sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{n-1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points outside \\(D\\) and \\(x+y=n\\).  \n   However the set \\(T_n\\setminus\\bigl(D\\cup\\{x+y=n\\}\\bigr)\\) contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-|D|-|x+y=n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n-(n-1)\n   =\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points – the same number. Thus *every* point of those anti‑diagonals must be hit by a sunny line. In particular, for each sum \\(s\\) the \\(s-1\\) points of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) must be distributed among the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, one point per line.  \n\n   Now fix two consecutive anti‑diagonals, say \\(x+y=s\\) and \\(x+y=s+1\\) (with \\(2\\le s\\le n-2\\)). Their points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\n   \\]\n   and  \n   \\[\n   (1,s),(2,s-1),\\dots ,(s,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The two points \\((i,s-i)\\) and \\((i,s+1-i)\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(i\\). If a sunny line contains \\((i,s-i)\\) it cannot also contain \\((i,s+1-i)\\) because that would make the line vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), which is forbidden. Consequently the two anti‑diagonals must be covered by **different** sunny lines for each value of \\(i\\). Since there are \\(s-1\\) values of \\(i\\), we need at least \\(s-1\\) distinct sunny lines to cover the two anti‑diagonals simultaneously.  \n\n   Performing this argument for all \\(s=2,4,6,\\dots ,n-1\\) (the even sums) we obtain a lower bound of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{\\substack{s=2\\\\ s\\text{ even}}}^{n-1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}4\n   \\]\n\n   distinct sunny lines. For odd \\(n\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}4 > n-1,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\((n-1)(n-2) > 4(n-1)\\) for every odd \\(n\\ge 3\\). This contradicts the assumption that only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are available. Hence for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-1\\text{ is possible iff }n\\text{ is even}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  \n   * For every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) the construction of §4 works, giving a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   * For even \\(n\\) the construction of §5 supplies a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\).  \n   * For odd \\(n\\) the impossibility proof of §5 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) cannot be achieved.\n\n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
