tains the even number \\(n-1\\) of points \\(P_i\\) (4). Because a sunny line cannot contain two points of the same anti‑diagonal, each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must contain **exactly one** of the points \\(P_i\\). Consequently every sunny line is determined by the choice of its point on \\(x+y=n\\).  \n\n   Consider the remaining points of \\(T_n\\), i.e. those not on \\(D\\) and not on the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n\\). They all lie on anti‑diagonals with sums \\(s\\) where \\(2\\le s\\le n-1\\). For a fixed \\(s\\) the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points, all of which have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates. Because the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines already have distinct slopes (they are forced to be distinct, otherwise two of them would meet the same point of \\(x+y=n\\) and would be identical), at most one of those lines can pass through a given point of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\). Hence the total number of points of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(s-1\\).  \n\n   Adding over all \\(s=2,\\dots ,n-1\\) we obtain that the sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{n-1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points outside \\(D\\) and \\(x+y=n\\).  \n   However the set \\(T_n\\setminus\\bigl(D\\cup\\{x+y=n\\}\\bigr)\\) contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-|D|-|x+y=n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n-(n-1)\n   =\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points – the same number. Thus *every* point of those anti‑diagonals must be hit by a sunny line. In particular, for each sum \\(s\\) the \\(s-1\\) points of the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) must be distributed among the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, one point per line.  \n\n   Now fix two consecutive anti‑diagonals, say \\(x+y=s\\) and \\(x+y=s+1\\) (with \\(2\\le s\\le n-2\\)). Their points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\n   \\]\n   and  \n   \\[\n   (1,s),(2,s-1),\\dots ,(s,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The two points \\((i,s-i)\\) and \\((i,s+1-i)\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(i\\). If a sunny line contains \\((i,s-i)\\) it cannot also contain \\((i,s+1-i)\\) because that would make the line vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), which is forbidden. Consequently the two anti‑diagonals must be covered by **different** sunny lines for each value of \\(i\\). Since there are \\(s-1\\) values of \\(i\\), we need at least \\(s-1\\) distinct sunny lines to cover the two anti‑diagonals simultaneously.  \n\n   Performing this argument for all \\(s=2,4,6,\\dots ,n-1\\) (the even sums) we obtain a lower bound of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{\\substack{s=2\\\\ s\\text{ even}}}^{n-1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}4\n   \\]\n\n   distinct sunny lines. For odd \\(n\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}4 > n-1,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\((n-1)(n-2) > 4(n-1)\\) for every odd \\(n\\ge 3\\). This contradicts the assumption that only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are available. Hence for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-1\\text{ is possible iff }n\\text{ is even}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  \n   * For every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) the construction of §4 works, giving a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   * For even \\(n\\) the construction of §5 supplies a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\).  \n   * For odd \\(n\\) the impossibility proof of §5 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) cannot be achieved.\n\n   Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer. ∎"
