verage.*  \n   \u2013 Every point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) is covered by the line (1).  \n   \u2013 For each \\(i\\le k\\) the point \\(Q_i\\) lies on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{i+2}\\); thus each anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{3},D_{4},\\dots ,D_{k+2}\\) is hit once.  \n   \u2013 The remaining anti\u2011diagonals \\(D_{2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n}\\) are taken as non\u2011sunny lines themselves.  \n\n   Counting the lines we have  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(D_{n+1})\\;+\\;k\\;(S_i)\\;+\\;(n-1-k)\\;( \\text{the other anti\u2011diagonals})=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **All sunny except one (\\(k=n-1\\)).**  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\).*  \n   Keep the line \\(D_{n+1}\\) as the unique non\u2011sunny line.  \n   The anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{n}\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,n-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Pair the points as \\((P_{2j-1},P_{2j})\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,\\frac{n-2}{2}\\) and treat the remaining point \\(P_{n-1}\\) separately.  \n   For each pair define a sunny line through the two points; its slope is an integer different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).  \n   The line through the unpaired point \\(P_{n-1}\\) and the point \\((1,2)\\) is also sunny.  \n   Thus we obtain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together with the single non\u2011sunny line \\(D_{n+1}\\); all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.  \n\n   *Odd \\(n\\) \u2013 impossibility.*  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line exists. By a suitable affine transformation we may assume that the non\u2011sunny line is the largest anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   The anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{n}\\) contains the even number \\(n-1\\) of points \\(P_i\\) defined above.  \n   Because a sunny line cannot contain two points of the same anti\u2011diagonal, each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must contain **exactly one** of the points \\(P_i\\).  \n\n   Consider any anti\u2011diagonal 