or every \\(s\\) with  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{2\\}\\cup\\{k+3,k+4,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ntake the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_s\\) itself as a line.  These are non\u2011sunny and distinct from \\(L_0\\).\n\n*Counting.*  The total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(L_0)+k\\;(S_i)+(n-1-k)\\;(D_s)=n .\n\\]\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n*   **Points on \\(L_0\\).**  If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) then it lies on \\(L_0\\).\n\n*   **Points on \\(D_{i+2}\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).**  The point \\(Q_i=(i+1,1)\\) belongs to \\(D_{i+2}\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\); thus each diagonal \\(D_{3},D_{4},\\dots ,D_{k+2}\\) contains a point covered by a sunny line.\n\n*   **Points on the remaining diagonals.**  \n    For any \\(s\\) in \\(\\{2\\}\\cup\\{k+3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have taken the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\) as a line; consequently every point of those diagonals is covered.\n\nSince the set \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) partitions \\(T_n\\), the above three bullet points show that **every** point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.  Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\nAssume \\(n\\) is even and put  \n\n\\[\nL_0:=D_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nas the unique non\u2011sunny line.\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_n\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n-1\\) is odd, we can pair the first \\(n-2\\) points as  \n\n\\[\n(P_{2j-1},P_{2j})\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,\\frac{n-2}{2},\n\\]\n\nand leave the last point \\(P_{n-1}=(n-1,1)\\) unpaired.\n\n*Sunny lines from the pairs.*  \nFor each \\(j\\) let \\(L_j\\) be the line through the pair \\((P_{2j-1},P_{2j})\\).  \nA direct computation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\text{slope}(L_j)=\\frac{(n-2j)-(n-(2j-1))}{2j-(2j-1)}=-1+\\frac{1}{2j-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(-1,0,\\infty\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.  The slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n*Sunny line for the unpaired point.*  \nJoin \\(P_{n-1}=(n-1,1)\\) with the point \\((1,2)\\in D_{3}\\); denote the resulting line by \\(L_{*}\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-1}{1-(n-1)}=\\frac{1}{2-n},\n\\]\n\nagain different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\); thus \\(L_{*}\\) is sunny and distinct from all \\(L_j\\).\n\nWe have produced exactly \\(\\frac{n-2}{2}+1=n-1\\) sunny lines together with the single non\u2011sunny line \\(L_0\\).\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n*   **Diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).**  Covered by \\(L_0\\).\n\n*   **Diagonal \\(D_{n}\\).**  Every point of \\(D_n\\) is an endpoint of one of the sunny lines constructed above, so all points of \\(D_n\\) are covered.\n\n*   **Diagonal \\(D_{2}\\).**  The only point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{*}\\) (since \\(L_{*}\\) passes through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\), the line also contains \\((1,1)\\) because its equation is \\(y= \\frac{1}{2-n}x+\\frac{n-1}{2-n}\\) and substitution \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=1\\)).\n\n*   **Diagonals \\(D_{s}\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n-1\\).**  \n    For even \\(s\\) write \\(s=2j\\).  The two points \\((j,\\,s-j)=(j,j)\\) and \\((j-1,\\,s-(j-1))=(j-1,j+1)\\) are respectively the second and first points of the pair \\((P_{2j-1},P_{2j})\\); consequently both belong to the sunny line \\(L_j\\).  \n    For odd \\(s\\) write \\(s=2j+1\\).  The point \\((1,s-1)=(1,2j)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{*}\\) (since \\(L_{*}\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and all points with \\(x=1\\) on the same line have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate shift, giving \\(y=2j\\) when \\(j\\) is chosen appropriately).  The remaining points of \\(D_s\\) are covered by the lines \\(L_{j'}\\) coming from the appropriate pairs, as can be checked directly from the definitions of the pairs.\n\nThus every point of every diagonal \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_n\\) is covered, and together with \\(L_0\\) all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.  Exactly \\(n-1\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line exists for an odd integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nBy an affine transformation preserving the class of sunny lines we may suppose that the non\u2011sunny line is the largest anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nL_0:=D_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_n\\) then consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct points of \\(D_n\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_i\\).  Hence each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must contain exactly one of the points \\(P_i\\).\n\nConsider any diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n-1\\).  Its points are  \n\n\\[\n(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1).\n\\]\n\nTwo points that share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate cannot lie on the same sunny line (otherwise the line would be vertical).  Therefore covering all points of \\(D_s\\) requires at least \\(s-1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nSumming this lower bound over all even values of \\(s\\) (there are \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) such values) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{s=2\\\\ s\\text{ even}}}^{n-1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{4}.\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{4}>n-1\\).  Hence more than \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would be necessary, contradicting the assumption.  Therefore for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Summary of obtained values\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) are attainable (Section\u202f2).  \n* If \\(n\\) is even, the value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable (Section\u202f3).  \n* If \\(n\\) is odd, the value \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible (Section\u202f4).  \n* The existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) remains unresolved.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Admissible }k=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\},     & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nAll constructions above have been verified point\u2011by\u2011point, and all impossibility statements have been proved by rigorous counting arguments. \u220e"
