ry point of the bottom row \\(y=1\\) is covered.  \n   * Every remaining point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) belongs to a unique pair \\(\\{A_j,B_{j'}\\}\\) used above, and therefore lies on the corresponding sunny line.  \n\n   Consequently all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(n-1\\) of the lines are sunny.  Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line exists for an odd integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   By an affine transformation preserving the three forbidden directions we may assume that the non‑sunny line is the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n\n   The diagonal \\(D_n\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\,n-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Any two distinct points of \\(D_n\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_i\\).  Hence each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must contain exactly one of the points \\(P_i\\).\n\n   Consider an anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) with even index \\(s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n-1\\)).  \n   Its points are \\((1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1)\\).  \n   Two points of the same column cannot lie on the same sunny line (otherwise the line would be vertical), and two points of the same row cannot lie on the same sunny line (otherwise the line would be horizontal).  Consequently covering all points of \\(D_s\\) requires at least \\(s-1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   Summing this lower bound over all even \\(s\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{\\substack{s=2\\\\ s\\text{ even}}}^{n-1}(s-1)\n   =\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n   For odd \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{4}>n-1\\); thus more than \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would be necessary, a contradiction.  Hence for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Assume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Then