n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_0&:=D_{n+1}\\;(x+y=n+1),\\\\\nL_1&:=D_{2},\\\\\nL_2&:=D_{k+3},\\;L_3:=D_{k+4},\\dots ,\\;\nL_{n-1-k}:=D_{n}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll these lines are anti‑diagonals, hence non‑sunny, and they are distinct.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i)\\in D_{n+1},\\qquad \nQ_i=(i+1,\\,1)\\in D_{i+2},\n\\]\nand let \\(S_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n\\le -2,\n\\]\nso \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  Because the numbers \\(i-n\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\), the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n1. **Points on \\(D_{n+1}\\).**  Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on \\(L_0\\).\n\n2. **Points on the diagonals \\(D_{3},D_{4},\\dots ,D_{k+2}\\).**  \n   For each \\(i\\le k\\) the point \\(Q_i=(i+1,1)\\) belongs to \\(D_{i+2}\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\); therefore each of the anti‑diagonals \\(D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+2}\\) contains at least one covered point.\n\n3. **Points on the remaining diagonals.**  \n   The anti‑diagonals \\(D_{2},D_{k+3},D_{k+4},\\dots ,D_{n}\\) are taken as whole lines \\(L_1,L_2,\\dots\\); consequently every point belonging to any of these diagonals is covered.\n\nSince the family \\(\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) partitions \\(T_n\\), the three bullets together guarantee that **every** point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.  Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n\nAssume \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny line.*  \n\\(L_0:=D_{n+1}\\;(x+y=n+1)\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nA_j=(j,\\,n+1-j),\\qquad \nB_j=(j+1,\\,1),\n\\]\nand let \\(L_j\\) be the line through \\(A_j\\) and \\(B_j\\).  \nIts slope is \\(j-n\\).  Because \\(n\\) is even, the numbers \\(j-n\\) (\\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) are all different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\); consequently each \\(L_j\\) is sunny and the \\(n-1\\) lines are distinct.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n* The non‑sunny line \\(L_0\\) covers all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n* For each \\(j\\) the point \\(B_j=(j+1,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\); therefore every point of the bottom row \\(y=1\\) is covered.  \n* Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\).  Write \\(a=j\\) for some \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\); then \\(b=n+1-j-(a-b)\\).  A short computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_j\\) (the line through \\(A_j\\) and \\(B_j\\)).  Hence every point with \\(b\\ge2\\) is also covered.\n\nThus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(n-1\\) of the lines are sunny.  Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line exists for an odd integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nBy an affine transformation preserving the three forbidden directions we may suppose that the non‑sunny line is the anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nL_0:D_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_n\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n-i)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct points of \\(D_n\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_i\\).  Hence each of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must contain exactly one of the points \\(P_i\\).\n\nConsider an anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) with even index \\(s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n-1\\)).  \nIts points are  \n\n\\[\n(1,s-1),(2,s-2),\\dots ,(s-1,1).\n\\]\n\nNo two of them share the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate or the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, so a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(D_s\\).  Therefore covering all points of \\(D_s\\) requires at least \\(s-1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nSumming this lower bound over all even \\(s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{s=2\\\\ s\\text{ even}}}^{n-1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{4}.\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{4}>n-1\\); consequently more than \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would be necessary, contradicting the assumption.  Hence for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nSuppose all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Then none of them is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  In particular a sunny line can contain at most one point of the set  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause the points of \\(B\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates.  Since \\(|B|=n\\), each of the \\(n\\) sunny lines must contain exactly one point of \\(B\\).  Consequently no line can contain any other point of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_n\\) be covered.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nCollecting the positive constructions (Sections 2 and 3) and the impossibility results (Sections 4 and 5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\ \\cup\\ \n\\begin{cases}\n\\{n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\varnothing, & n\\text{ odd}\n\\end{cases}\n\\;}\n\\]\n\nas the complete set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.  ∎"
