\n   *Coverage.*  \n   – Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n   – If \\(a-b=i-1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) because \\(b=a-i+1\\).  \n   – If \\(a-b\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\(a\\ge k+1\\); consequently the point lies in column \\(a\\) and is covered by the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line exists.  \n   By an affine transformation preserving the three forbidden directions we may assume that the non‑sunny line is vertical, say \\(x=c\\).  \n   The column \\(x=c\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,n)\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Each sunny line can contain **at most one** of these points, because two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate would force the line to be vertical (non‑sunny).  \n   Since there are only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, at most \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) points of the column can be covered – a contradiction.  \n   Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) does not exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Assume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   As above, a sunny line can contain at most one point of any fixed column.  \n   The column \\(x=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines to cover that column, which forces every line to pass through exactly one of those points.  \n   Consequently each line is determined solely by its point in column 1; it cannot pass through any other point of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise it would contain two points of the same column).  \n   Thus the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) would be uncovered – a contradiction.  \n   Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\