   There are \\(n\\) points in \\(C_1\\).  \n   Suppose a configuration has exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   By the lemma, the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\).  \n   The remaining point of \\(C_1\\) must be covered by a non\u2011sunny line, i.e. a vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   But the line \\(x=1\\) would then be the **same** as the vertical line already used for that column, and we would have two distinct lines coinciding, contradicting the requirement that the \\(n\\) lines be distinct.  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) cannot exist.\n\n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the same argument applied to the column \\(x=1\\) shows that at most \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) can be covered, but we would need a distinct sunny line for each of the \\(n\\) points.  \n   Two different sunny lines cannot both pass through the same point \\((1,b)\\) because then they would coincide (two distinct lines intersect in at most one point).  \n   Consequently we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines **and** a further line to cover the remaining points of the triangle, which is impossible with only \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does not exist.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n**Notation.**  \n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\). All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\nEach \\(L_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain **no** sunny line.  \n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nStart with the \\(n-2\\) anti\u2011diagonal lines  \n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\nThese lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except those belonging to the two topmost anti\u2011diagonals\n\\[\nD_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n\\},\\qquad D_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the two sunny lines\n\\[\nS_1:\\; y=\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n}{2},\\qquad\nS_2:\\; y=-\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n+2}{2}.\n\\]\nBoth have slopes \\(\\pm\\frac12\\), which are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(D_n\\).*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in D_n\\) we have \\(b=n-a\\). Substituting into the equation of \\(S_1\\) gives\n\\[\nn-a=\\frac12 a+\\frac{n}{2}\\Longleftrightarrow a=n,\n\\]\nwhich is impossible because \\(a\\le n-1\\) on \\(D_n\\).  \nThus no point of \\(D_n\\) lies on \\(S_1\\).  \nSimilarly, substituting into \\(S_2\\) yields\n\\[\nn-a=-\\frac12 a+\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Longleftrightarrow a=2,\n\\]\nso the only point of \\(D_n\\) that could belong to \\(S_2\\) is \\((2,n-2)\\), which indeed satisfies \\(2+(n-2)=n\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(D_n\\) is covered either by the line \\(L_n\\) (which we have **not** kept) or by \\(S_2\\); in our set we keep \\(S_2\\), so the whole diagonal \\(D_n\\) is covered.\n\n*Coverage of \\(D_{n+1}\\).*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in D_{n+1}\\) we have \\(b=n+1-a\\). Substituting into \\(S_1\\) gives\n\\[\nn+1-a=\\frac12 a+\\frac{n}{2}\\Longleftrightarrow a=2,\n\\]\nso the point \\((2,n-1)\\) lies on \\(S_1\\).  \nSubstituting into \\(S_2\\) yields\n\\[\nn+1-a=-\\frac12 a+\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Longleftrightarrow a=3,\n\\]\nso the point \\((3,n-2)\\) lies on \\(S_2\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) have abscissae \\(a\\ge4\\) or \\(a=1\\); none of them satisfy either of the two equalities, hence they are not on \\(S_1\\) nor on \\(S_2\\).  \nThese points are covered by the anti\u2011diagonal line \\(L_{n+1}\\), which we have **not** kept.  \nTo avoid using \\(L_{n+1}\\) we simply **delete** the sunny line \\(S_2\\) and **add** the missing anti\u2011diagonal line \\(L_{n+1}\\).  \n\nThe resulting family of \\(n\\) distinct lines consists of  \n\n* the \\(n-2\\) anti\u2011diagonal lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\);  \n* the single sunny line \\(S_1\\);  \n* the anti\u2011diagonal line \\(L_{n+1}\\).\n\nIt contains exactly **one** sunny line and, by the case analysis above, covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nConsider the leftmost column \\(x=1\\); it contains the \\(n\\) points\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Lemma.* A sunny line meets the column \\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  \n*Proof.* If a sunny line contained two distinct points of the form \\((1,b_1)\\) and \\((1,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\), the line would be vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e  \n\nSince there are only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, they can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\).  \nThe remaining point of \\(C_1\\) must be covered by a non\u2011sunny line.  \nThe only non\u2011sunny lines that meet the column \\(x=1\\) are the vertical line \\(x=1\\) itself and possibly a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (which meets the column at a single point).  \nIf the covering non\u2011sunny line is horizontal, it meets the column at exactly one point, leaving the other points of the column uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nThus the covering non\u2011sunny line must be the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nBut the vertical line \\(x=1\\) coincides with any other line that also passes through a point of \\(C_1\\); consequently two of the \\(n\\) lines would be identical, violating the requirement that the lines be distinct.  \n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nSuppose all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nAgain look at the column \\(x=1\\) containing the \\(n\\) points \\(C_1\\).  \nBy the lemma above, each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C_1\\); therefore we need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines to cover all points of \\(C_1\\).  \nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, each line must contain **exactly one** point of \\(C_1\\).  \n\nTake two different points of \\(C_1\\), say \\((1,b_1)\\) and \\((1,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\).  \nThe unique sunny line that contains \\((1,b_1)\\) cannot contain any other point of \\(C_1\\); consequently it cannot pass through any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b=b_1\\), because then the line would intersect the column \\(x=1\\) at two distinct points, which is impossible for a straight line.  \nThus each sunny line meets the set \\(T_n\\) only in the single point of \\(C_1\\) that it was assigned.  \n\nAll remaining points of \\(T_n\\) (those with \\(a\\ge2\\)) are therefore uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.  \n\nHence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nWe have proved rigorously that\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable;  \n* \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are never attainable.\n\nThe status of the intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) remains open. \u220e"

