ine meets the column \\(x=1\\) in at most one point. \u2026\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** Correct. A sunny line cannot be vertical, so it can intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) at most once.\n\n#### 4.2. Counting argument\n> \u201cSince there are only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, they can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\). The remaining point of \\(C_1\\) must be covered by a non\u2011sunny line.\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** This reasoning is sound.\n\n#### 4.3. Claim about horizontal non\u2011sunny line\n> \u201cIf the covering non\u2011sunny line is horizontal, it meets the column at exactly one point, leaving the other points of the column uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\u201d\n\n- **Issue:** The contradiction does **not** follow. The sunny lines already cover the other \\(n-1\\) points of the column; a horizontal line covering the single remaining point is perfectly consistent.  \n- **Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the argument incorrectly dismisses a viable possibility.\n\n#### 4.4. Claim about vertical non\u2011sunny line\n> \u201cThus the covering non\u2011sunny line must be the vertical line \\(x=1\\). \u2026 the vertical line \\(x=1\\) coincides with any other line that also passes through a point of \\(C_1\\); consequently two of the \\(n\\) lines would be identical, violating the requirement that the lines be distinct.\u201d\n\n- **Issue:** Two distinct lines can intersect at a point; a vertical line \\(x=1\\) does **not** coincide with any other line that merely shares a point of \\(C_1\\). The statement \u201ccoincides\u201d is false.  \n- **Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the reasoning that forces a contradiction is invalid.\n\n#### 4.5. Overall conclusion for \\(k=n-1\\)\n> \u201cHence a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) cannot exist.\u201d\n\n- **Issue:** Since the impossibility proof contains critical logical errors, the conclusion is unsupported.  \n- **Classification:** **Critical Error** (the result is not proven).\n