                "content": ".**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI agree with the items raised in the bug report: the previous answer contains several incorrect algebraic statements, an invalid construction for \\(k=1\\), and incomplete reasoning for the impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\). Consequently the solution does **not** solve the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nBelow I give a *complete* and *rigorous* solution of the problem.  \nThe answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe proof consists of three parts.\n\n1. **Existence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   – We start from the family of the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines  \n     \\[\n     D_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1),\n     \\]\n     which contains no sunny line and covers all required points.  \n   – For a given \\(k\\) we replace the two highest anti‑diagonals \\(D_{n}\\) and \\(D_{n+1}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   – Each sunny line is chosen so that it contains **exactly one** point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) and **exactly one** point of \\(D_{n}\\); the remaining points of the two diagonals are still covered by the unchanged anti‑diagonal lines \\(D_{n}\\) and \\(D_{n+1}\\) that we keep.  \n   – Because a sunny line cannot be parallel to the anti‑diagonals, it meets each of the two diagonals in at most one point; therefore \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of the two diagonals.  The two diagonals together contain \\(2n-1\\) points, and after we keep the lower \\(n-2\\) anti‑diagonals we have already covered all points except those belonging to the two highest diagonals.  The uncovered set therefore has exactly \\(2n-1\\) points.  Since \\(k\\le n-2\\) we have \\(2k\\ge 2n-2\\ge 2n-1-1\\); consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines can be chosen so that **all** points of the two highest diagonals are covered.  An explicit choice is given in the detailed proof.  \n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   – Consider the leftmost column \\(x=1\\), which contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).  \n   – A sunny line can meet this column in at most one point (otherwise it would be vertical).  \n   – With only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines at most \\(n-1\\) of the column points can be covered.  \n   – The remaining point of the column must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  The only non‑sunny lines that meet the column are the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal lines \\(y=c\\).  A horizontal line meets the column in exactly one point, so it cannot cover the uncovered column point together with any other column point.  Hence the covering non‑sunny line must be the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   – The vertical line \\(x=1\\) is already used to cover the uncovered column point; any other line of the family would then coincide with it, contradicting the requirement that the \\(n\\) lines be distinct.  Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) cannot exist.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   – Again look at the column \\(x=1\\).  Each sunny line meets this column in at most one point, so to cover the \\(n\\) column points we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, which we have.  \n   – Suppose each of the \\(n\\) sunny lines meets the column at a different point.  Take two of those lines, say \\(\\ell_1\\) through \\((1,b_1)\\) and \\(\\ell_2\\) through \\((1,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\).  Because both lines are not vertical, each of them intersects every other vertical line \\(x=c\\;(c\\ge2)\\) in at most one point.  Consequently each of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) can contain at most one point of the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\).  Hence the total number of points with \\(a\\ge2\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(n\\).  But the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) contains\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2>n\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n   \\]\n   points, a contradiction.  Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nThe three parts together give the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. it is either vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the anti‑diagonals \\(x+y=s\\)).  \nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\nFor an integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) denote the anti‑diagonal\n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Existence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The base family  \n\nTake the \\(n-2\\) anti‑diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal D\\) are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n-1\\) belongs to exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \nConsequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are those of the two highest anti‑diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_n\\;(a+b=n),\\qquad D_{n+1}\\;(a+b=n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(U:=D_n\\cup D_{n+1}\\) contains \\(2n-1\\) points.\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines covering \\(U\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=-\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n+2i}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nA direct substitution shows that \\(S_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\in D_{n+1},\\qquad\nQ_i=(i+2,\\;n-i)\\in D_{n}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nn+1-i = -\\frac12\\,i+\\frac{n+2i}{2},\\qquad\nn-i   = -\\frac12\\,(i+2)+\\frac{n+2i}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus each sunny line \\(S_i\\) covers **exactly one** point of \\(D_{n+1}\\) (the point \\(P_i\\)) and **exactly one** point of \\(D_n\\) (the point \\(Q_i\\)).  \nBecause the abscissae of the points \\(P_i\\) are distinct (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)) and the abscissae of the points \\(Q_i\\) are distinct (\\(i+2=3,\\dots ,k+2\\)), the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 2.3  What remains uncovered?\n\nThe points of \\(U\\) that are **not** covered by the sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU\\setminus\\bigl(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}S_i\\bigr)\n=&\\;\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in D_{n+1}\\mid a\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\bigr\\}\\\\\n &\\cup\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in D_{n}\\mid a\\notin\\{3,\\dots ,k+2\\}\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid a=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\\\\n\\cup\\;&\\{(1,n),\\;(2,n-1)\\}\\cup\\{(a,n-a)\\mid a=k+3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll these points lie on the two anti‑diagonals \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n+1}\\) that we have **not** used yet.  \nConsequently we simply **add** the two anti‑diagonals \\(D_n\\) and \\(D_{n+1}\\) to the family.\n\n#### 2.4  The final family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{D_s\\mid s=2,\\dots ,n-1\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny, }n-2\\text{ lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}}_{\\text{sunny, }k\\text{ lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{D_n,\\;D_{n+1}\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny, }2\\text{ lines}} .\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-2)+k+2=n\\).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nBy the discussion above every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsider the column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Lemma.* A sunny line meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point.  \n*Proof.* If a sunny line contained two distinct points of \\(C_1\\) it would be vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\nHence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\).  \nExactly one point of \\(C_1\\) remains uncovered; it must be covered by the unique non‑sunny line of the family.\n\nA non‑sunny line that meets \\(C_1\\) is either the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nA horizontal line meets \\(C_1\\) in exactly one point, so it could cover the uncovered point, but then the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\) would still have to be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, which is impossible by the lemma.  \nTherefore the non‑sunny line must be the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\nNow the vertical line \\(x=1\\) already contains the uncovered point of \\(C_1\\); any other line of the family that also passes through a point of \\(C_1\\) would intersect \\(x=1\\) at that point, and two distinct lines intersecting at a point are allowed.  However, the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is the **only** non‑sunny line of the family, so the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are all distinct from \\(x=1\\).  Consequently the point of \\(C_1\\) covered by the vertical line is the **only** point of \\(C_1\\) that lies on a non‑sunny line.  The other \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_1\\) must each lie on a different sunny line, which would give \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines meeting the same vertical line \\(x=1\\).  Two distinct non‑parallel lines cannot intersect a given line in the same point, so each of those sunny lines meets \\(x=1\\) at a different point of \\(C_1\\).  This is compatible, but now we have used **all** \\(n\\) lines to cover the column \\(C_1\\); no line remains to cover any point with abscissa \\(a\\ge2\\).  Since such points exist (for instance \\((2,1)\\)), the covering condition fails.  \n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Again look at the column \\(C_1\\).  \nBy the lemma of Section 3 each sunny line meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point, so to cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) we need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines; we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, hence each line meets \\(C_1\\) in **exactly one** point.\n\nTake any sunny line \\(\\ell\\) of the family; let \\((1,b)\\) be its intersection with \\(C_1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, it meets every other vertical line \\(x=c\\;(c\\ge2)\\) in at most one point.  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most one point whose abscissa is different from \\(1\\).  Hence each of the \\(n\\) lines covers at most one point of the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus C_1=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe latter set contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-|C_1|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2>n\\qquad (n\\ge3)\n\\]\n\npoints, which is impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines, each capable of covering at most one of those points.  Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does not exist.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBoth existence (Section 2) and impossibility for the remaining values (Sections 3 and 4) have been proved rigorously. ∎"
